The best approach is to use the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package rather than rolling your own wrapper. It's battle-tested and covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, and more.

**1. Install the packages:**

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`):**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool FolderExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

Assert.True(service.FolderExists("/data"));
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Read("/data/config.json"));

service.Write("/data/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));
```

**Why this over a custom wrapper?**
- Covers the full `System.IO` surface — you won't need to expand it later.
- `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem out of the box — no mocking framework needed.
- Registration is a single `AddSingleton` call (the wrapper is stateless).

Once the package is in place, the next step is updating each call site to use `fileSystem.File.*` / `fileSystem.Directory.*` instead of the static `File.*` / `Directory.*`. If you have many call sites to migrate, I can help with that too.
